commit dab4b8a6dded7a561f486631ac0e93b787d9d96a
Author: authorss81 <authorss81@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Tue Oct 13 12:01:15 2026 +0530

    rbops: 20 phases for stdlib substance, and gate the REPL behind them
    
    Six questions were answered about where Redblue goes next. The answer
    that came out of all six is the same: the standard library is the
    ceiling, not the REPL.
    
    VERIFIED, not assumed. src/stdlib.rs:612 says 'Redblue has no regex,
    so this is a literal separator' - the regex crate is a dependency used
    only by src/testing/assertions.rs:130 and src/testing/runner.rs:252, so
    it reaches the test harness and nothing else. No date value exists, only
    time.now/sleep/format/unix. No pathlib, no argparse, no logging, no
    encodings, no numeric container. src/value.rs:276 shows the memory
    model is Arc-shared and copy-on-read with no Weak anywhere, which means
    a cycle leaks and there is no collector - worth writing down rather than
    leaving to be rediscovered as a mystery.
    
    090-109, in two tiers.
    
    Tier 0 (090-094) derives the work rather than guessing it. 090 surveys
    the gaps with file:line evidence and ranks them by how many programs
    each blocks; 094 turns that into LIBRARIES.md, the standing policy on
    what to write in Redblue, what to bind to a Rust crate, and what not to
    build - so no future phase re-argues it. 091 documents the memory model
    including the cycle leak. 092/093 shard and tier CI: 1,472 #[test]
    functions and 722 corpus files in one serial job is a ceiling on how
    much the language is allowed to test.
    
    Tier 1 (095-108) is the stdlib itself: regex, then date and time split
    across value and parsing because they are separable units of work, then
    itertools/functools, collections, paths, text, CLI parsing, logging,
    encodings, formats, a numpy-style array binding, statistics with exact
    decimals, and package/language version independence.
    
    THE ANSWER TO 'CAN REDBLUE HAVE PYTORCH' IS IN 094 AND 106. It cannot,
    written in Redblue, and not for want of effort: tensor and autograd work
    is fast only when native, which is exactly why numpy and PyTorch are C++
    and not Python. An interpreted Redblue is slower than Python. The answer
    is a binding, the same one Python reached - and Redblue already binds
    crates this way, with network.get wrapping reqwest.
    
    Each tier-1 entry is pre-split to one coherent unit, because a phase
    that discovers halfway through that it is three phases has burned an
